s=s[:a]+r'''3.1 If $K_+$ is nonempty, choose $a'>a$ with $K_+\subset I^+_M(S_{a'})$, as follows. The opens $I^+_M(S_c)$ for $c>a$ cover $I^+_M(S_a)$: a timelike curve from $S_a$ to a point of this latter set has a short initial segment inside the open $U$, on which $t_U$ increases above $a$ and crosses some level $c>a$ before reaching the endpoint. For a finite subcover of the compact $K_+$ choose $a'$ between $a$ and the least selected $c$; ordering of the levels then gives the asserted containment. This never evaluates $t_U$ outside $U$. The sets $A:=J^-_M(S_a)$ and $B:=J^+_M(S_{a'})$ are closed and disjoint: any causal segment from the later level to the earlier one would stay in $U$ and contradict strict increase of $t_U$. A smooth partition for $M\setminus A$ and $M\setminus B$ gives $\rho_+:M\to[0,1]$ equal to zero near $A$ and one near $B$, with $\operatorname{supp}\rho_+\subset M\setminus A$ and $\operatorname{supp}(d\rho_+)\subset M\setminus(A\cup B)$. Every point in that transition set is causally between $S_a$ and $S_{a'}$, because both are Cauchy sets of $M$; causal convexity puts this transition set inside $U$. The larger shadow $H_+:=J^+_M(S_a)\cap J^-_M(K_+)$ is compact in $M$: cover $K_+$ by finitely many open chronological pasts $I^-_M(q_j)$, use transitivity to enclose $H_+$ in $\bigcup_j(J^-(q_j)\cap J^+(S_a))$, compact by [F9], and use closedness from [F2] and the compact-set closedness underlying [F3]. The shadow need not lie in $U$. Put $\psi_+:=\rho_+E_-f_+$. Its closed support lies in $H_+$, so $\psi_+\in\mathcal D_g(M)$. Since $\rho_+=1$ near $K_+$, the product rule gives $g_+:=f_+-P\psi_+=-[P,\rho_+]E_-f_+$, with $[P,\rho]u:=P(\rho u)-\rho Pu$. Its support lies in the transition set inside $U$ and in the compact shadow $H_+$, so $g_+$ has compact support in $U$. By [F8], $Eg_+=Ef_+$. If $K_+=\varnothing$, set $g_+=0$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, F8, F9, step 1.1, step 2.1, given, construct]

4.1 The time-reversed construction applies to $K_-\subset I^-_M(S_b)$. In detail, the opens $I^-_M(S_c)$ for $c<b$ cover $I^-_M(S_b)$ by taking a short terminal timelike segment inside $U$; a finite subcover selects $b'<b$ with $K_-\subset I^-_M(S_{b'})$. A partition for the complements of the closed disjoint sets $A':=J^-_M(S_{b'})$ and $B':=J^+_M(S_b)$ gives $\rho_-=1$ near $A'$, $\rho_-=0$ near $B'$, and $\operatorname{supp}\rho_-\subset M\setminus B'$. Its derivative support lies causally between the two levels and hence inside $U$. The shadow $H_-:=J^+_M(K_-)\cap J^-_M(S_b)$ is compact by the reversed [F9], a finite cover of $K_-$ by chronological futures, and closedness, just as in step 3.1. Define $\psi_-:=\rho_-E_+f_-\in\mathcal D_g(M)$ and $g_-:=f_--P\psi_-=-[P,\rho_-]E_+f_-$. The support of $g_-$ is closed, contained in $H_-$ and in the transition set inside $U$, so it is compact in $U$; [F8] gives $Eg_-=Ef_-$. The zero-support case uses $g_-=0$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, F8, F9, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, given, construct]'''+s[b:]
